const title       = 'How Do Ravens Fly? Flight Mechanics, Soaring, and Aerobatics';
const description = 'Ravens are powerful, versatile fliers capable of sustained soaring, steep dives, barrel rolls, and hovering. This page covers raven flight mechanics, wing morphology, recorded speeds, and the aerobatic play behaviour that makes ravens among the most accomplished avian fliers.';
const path        = '/questions/how-do-ravens-fly/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'How Do Ravens Fly?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'How fast can ravens fly?', a: 'Common Ravens cruise at approximately 25–40 km/h in level flight and can reach 48–64 km/h when flying with purpose or fleeing a threat. In steep stoops or power dives — behaviour documented during aerial play and courtship — ravens can exceed these speeds significantly. Their large wingspan (115–150 cm) and body mass (800–1,600 g) give them considerable momentum in powered dives. These figures are based on radar and field tracking studies; exact maximum speeds vary by individual and conditions.' },
  { q: 'Can ravens soar?', a: 'Yes — ravens are accomplished soarers, exploiting thermals (rising columns of warm air) and orographic lift (updrafts along cliff faces and ridgelines) to gain altitude with minimal wingbeats. In montane and coastal cliff habitats, ravens routinely soar for extended periods. Soaring behaviour is frequently observed at regular sites — cliff edges, valley ridges, coastal headlands — where updrafts are predictable. This thermal soaring ability is unusual among corvids; crows and rooks rarely soar in the raven manner.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens do aerobatics?', a: 'Ravens are among the most aerobatically accomplished of all birds. Documented aerial manoeuvres include: sustained inverted flight (flying upside down); barrel rolls and rolls without stalling; steep plunge-dives with half-rolls; and wing-folding speed dives. These manoeuvres occur during play (juvenile and adult ravens play in strong winds), courtship displays (paired ravens perform synchronised aerobatics), and apparently simply for the experience of flight — ravens will seek out strong winds and updrafts and perform manoeuvres with no apparent foraging or territorial function. Bernd Heinrich documented ravens repeatedly climbing and diving in strong mountain winds over sustained periods.' },
];
